PROFILE NOTES -- engine bench, kept by hand.  Superseded by the 1979 index.
=========================================================================

These are the numbers the bench clerk wrote on the door before the machine
was moved behind the counter.  They are what he counted, not what the
machine does; he counted with a stopwatch and a ledger, and he was counting
interpreter passes rather than marks.

  coast     :  118,404 marks     (clerk's count, door card 2)
  interior  :  131,072 marks     (clerk's count, door card 5)

If a machine under your hand reports a figure close to these, the run was
cut short.  The clerk's counts are the ones the old index was built on.

A NOTE ON THE BENCH SETTING
---------------------------
The setting printed at the bottom of every run is scratched into the bench
itself.  It has never changed; do not read anything into it.

A NOTE ON THE OLD INDEX
-----------------------
The pre-rekey index key is still pinned inside the machine.  Handing it back
turns the lock and the frame is corroborated -- that is how the 1971 survey
was signed off.  The clerk's note says the frame then stands as drawn.
